
Step 2 — Spring container is created

You are saying:

"Spring, create an ApplicationContext for me, and use beans.xml as your configuration."

So:

new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext("beans.xml")

creates the Spring IoC container.

Think:

                 Spring Container
                       │
                       │
                  reads beans.xml
                       │
                       ▼

The container is basically the place where Spring will create and manage objects (beans).

Step 3 — Spring goes to beans.xml

Spring looks inside:

src/main/resources/beans.xml

It finds:

<bean id="dev" class="org.raman.Dev">
</bean>

Now Spring understands:

"There is a bean called dev, and its class is org.raman.Dev."

So Spring knows which class it needs to create an object from.

Step 4 — Spring creates the Dev object

Spring sees:

class="org.raman.Dev"

So internally, conceptually, Spring does something similar to:

Dev obj = new Dev();

But you did not write new Dev().

Spring did it.

That's one of the important ideas behind IoC (Inversion of Control).

Normally:

Dev obj = new Dev();

means:

"I, the programmer, am creating the object."

With Spring:

<bean id="dev" class="org.raman.Dev">

means:

"Spring, you create and manage this object."

Step 5 — Spring keeps that object inside the container

Conceptually:

        Spring IoC Container
        ┌───────────────────┐
        │                   │
        │  "dev" → Dev obj  │
        │                   │
        └───────────────────┘

The important thing here is:

"dev"

is the bean ID.

And:

Dev obj

is the actual object.

So:

Bean ID             Object
  dev       ──────► Dev object
Step 6 — Now your next line executes

After the container has been created:

Dev obj = (Dev) context.getBean("dev");

You're saying:

"Container, give me the object whose bean ID is dev."

Spring looks inside itself:

Spring Container


"dev" ───────► Dev object

It finds it and gives the object back.

So now:

Dev obj

refers to the same Dev object that Spring created.

Step 7 — You call the method

Finally:

obj.code();

You're simply calling the code() method on that object.
